<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="contribute-an-injection-point" title="Contribute an injection point">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            Continues <a href="write-a-plugin.topic">Write a plugin</a>. A factory is registered
            from <code>contribute</code>, so your plugin has to load first.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        Give an <code>@At</code> an identifier of your own. Decide where in a method it lands.
        This one answers <code>acme:AFTER_LOGGING</code> with every call whose owner is
        <code>java.util.logging.Logger</code>.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="java" src="AcmePoints.java"/>

    <chapter title="What each method answers" id="methods">
        <code-block lang="java" src="AfterLogging.java"/>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="namespace() and ids()">
                <p>
                    The weaver reads both while it is built. It asks for the namespace once and
                    for the identifiers twice. Every identifier you offer carries your namespace.
                    One that does not costs the factory all of them —
                    <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the grammar and
                    the refusal.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="create(id)">
                <p>
                    The engine calls this once per resolution, and caches nothing it hands back.
                    A resolution is one <code>@At</code> against one method. Return a constant.
                    The identifier is the spelling the declaration wrote. A factory that switches
                    on it must answer for its retired spellings too.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="targetRequirement()">
                <p>
                    The point answers <code>FORBIDDEN</code>: it finds its own positions. A
                    declaration that writes <code>target</code> anyway is refused with
                    <code>AW1043</code>, before <code>find</code> runs. Drop the target, or answer
                    <code>REQUIRED</code> and read <code>spec.rawTarget()</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="supportsShift(shift)">
                <p>
                    The engine asks once per resolution here too. The shift comes as written,
                    <code>NONE</code> included. A refusal is <code>AW1102</code>, again before
                    <code>find</code>. The author drops <code>shift</code> from the
                    <code>@At</code>, or names a different point.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="find(method, code, spec, reporter)">
                <p>
                    Answer with positions in the view you were handed. Order them as an ordinal
                    should count them. One instance serves every weave, and a loader weaves
                    several classes at once. Keep nothing across a call —
                    <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Runtime weaving</a> has that loader.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What the engine does around find" id="pipeline">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["A declaration names acme:AFTER_LOGGING"] --> B{"Target and shift accepted?"}
    B -->|no| R["Refused before your code runs"]
    B -->|yes| C["Slice narrows the body"]
    C --> D["find hands back sites"]
    D --> E["Indices translated into the whole method"]
    E --> F["Ordinal picks one"]
    F --> G["Shift moves it"]
    G --> H["Unsafe positions dropped"]
    H --> I["The injector emits there"]
        </code-block>

        <list>
            <li><p>An index counts elements of <code>code.elements()</code>, not bytecode offsets.
                Labels, line numbers and local-variable entries are elements as well.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>Site.Kind.AFTER_ELEMENT</code> is what moves a position past the
                instruction you matched. Add one to the index yourself and it lands an element too
                far.</p></li>
            <li><p>An ordinal counts inside the slice, which is all you were shown. Narrowing
                belongs to the engine. A point that applies the ordinal applies it twice.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Reach it from a weave" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Register the factory from <code>contribute</code>. Everything else about the class
                is <a href="write-a-plugin.topic">Write a plugin</a>.</p>
            <code-block lang="java" src="AcmePlugin.java"/>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Write a target that logs, and a weave naming the identifier through
                <code>custom</code>. <a href="annotations.topic">Annotations</a> has the rest of
                the <code>@At</code> elements.</p>
            <code-block lang="java" src="Service.java"/>
            <code-block lang="java" src="Audited.java"/>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Compile the two of them in one round. The processor reports <code>AW1200</code>,
                which asks nothing of you. It leaves the identifier to weave time.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
javac -proc:full -cp "lib/*" -d plugins \
      src/fixture/Service.java src/fixture/Audited.java
]]></code-block>
            <p>Compile the weave alone, against a <code>Service.class</code> the processor can
                read, and <code>AW1101</code> comes back instead. The processor resolves the
                built-in points and no others — <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin
                can do</a>. Run one javac over the weave and its target together.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Turn the logger on. The call you match then prints something of its own.</p>
            <code-block lang="properties" src="logging.properties"/>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Build a weaver that carries your plugin, and load the target through it. Keep
                <path>plugins</path> off the classpath you pass.</p>
            <code-block lang="java" src="Logged.java"/>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
javac -cp "lib/*" -d classes \
      src/acme/AcmePoints.java src/acme/AfterLogging.java \
      src/acme/AcmePlugin.java src/launcher/Logged.java

java -Djava.util.logging.config.file=logging.properties \
     -cp "classes:lib/*" launcher.Logged 2>&1
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Three lines, with <code>audited</code> between the two the target prints. The site
                landed after the logger call. Before it, <code>audited</code> would print
                first.</p>
            <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
starting
audited
running
]]></code-block>
            <note>
                <p>Two lines instead of three, with <code>AW1101</code> and <code>AW1043</code>
                    above them. The weaver was built without your plugin. Give
                    <code>Weaver.builder()</code> the plugin, or check the identifier for a
                    typo.</p>
            </note>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="When the method has nothing you match" id="empty">
        <p>
            Answer with an empty list. That is not an error by itself. The declaration decides
            what it becomes.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="The declaration leaves require out">
                <p>The parser makes it 1, so an empty list is <code>AW1043</code>. Widen what you
                    match, or have the author write <code>require = 0</code>.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="The declaration names a group">
                <p>Nothing reads <code>require</code> at all. The matches are accounted against
                    the <a href="annotations.topic#weave"><code>@Group</code></a> the run declares.
                    Only the group's own bounds can fail.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Report why, too. The sample raises <code>acme:NO_LOGGING</code> beside that refusal,
            saying which method held nothing to match.
            <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a> covers the codes you may raise
            and what a throw costs. One throw is not caught: a bound reached from a built-in
            <code>@At</code> — <a href="reporting.topic#gaps">Where the guard does not
            reach</a>.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                A bound is handed <code>Reporter.NOOP</code>. A report you make while resolving
                one goes nowhere. The engine raises <code>AW1120</code> or <code>AW1121</code>
                itself. Each names the method and the bound, never the slice. Give the bound a
                site to land on, or it is refused.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

